1790607030.427	
1790607030.427	The history of human civilization is inextricably linked to the power of information. Before the advent of mechanical reproduction, knowledge was a painstakingly crafted commodity—hand-copied in monasteries, transcribed by scribes, and preserved in fragile manuscripts. This bottleneck in communication severely restricted intellectual growth, centralized authority among those who controlled literacy and scriptoria, and perpetuated societal structures defined by scarcity of access to ideas. The arrival of the printing press did not merely improve efficiency; it acted as a seismic ontological shift, fundamentally reordering politics, religion, science, economics, and culture across the globe.
1790607030.427	
1790607030.427	The story of the printing press is not a single narrative but a confluence of technological innovation, cultural need, and economic ambition stretching over two millennia. It is a saga moving from ancient woodblock carving to Gutenberg's revolutionary movable type, from manual artisan craft to mechanized industrial powerhouse—a journey that echoes humanity’s enduring desire to share, record, and evolve. To trace its history is to chart the trajectory of modernity itself.
