1790607030.427	
1790607030.427	In East Asia, particularly China, the earliest recognizable printing techniques emerged as far back as the Tang Dynasty (618–907 CE). These methods relied on stamping—the precursor to true printing. **Woodblock printing (xylography)** was perfected first. A text or image was carved in reverse onto a single block of wood, inked, and then pressed onto paper or silk. This method was labor-intensive because an entire page had to be carved into one monolithic block, meaning that if the printer wished to change even one word, the entire block had to be recarved—a monumental task. Despite its inherent limitations in speed and flexibility compared to later systems, woodblock printing allowed for the mass production of Buddhist texts, official decrees, and popular literature across Asia.
1790607030.427	
1790607030.427	While East Asia maintained a powerful tradition of printing, different languages (like Chinese logographic scripts) were better suited to the laborious fixed-type methodology of xylography than the alphabet-based languages prevalent in Europe. However, these ancient practices established crucial conceptual frameworks: the idea of an intermediary mechanism that could separate the message from its manual conveyance.
